WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.428
Managed secret stores move sensitive values out of source code and ordinary configuration files.

2
00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:16.419
Common options include Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, cloud key vault services, Kubernetes integrations, and platform-provided injection mechanisms.

3
00:00:16.419 --> 00:00:32.063
The names differ, but the engineering concerns are similar: where is the secret stored, how does the workload authenticate, what permissions does it receive, how is access audited, and what happens when the provider is slow or unavailable.

4
00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:39.188
There are two broad delivery models. In a pull model, the Go service calls a provider API at startup or runtime.

5
00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:45.074
It authenticates with a workload identity, requests a named secret, and receives a value.

6
00:00:45.074 --> 00:00:52.122
Pull models can support dynamic credentials and explicit refresh logic, but they add a runtime dependency.

7
00:00:52.122 --> 00:01:03.042
Your code must use context deadlines, handle cancellation, limit retries, and decide whether a missing provider is a startup failure or a degraded runtime condition.

8
00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:12.645
In an injection model, the platform delivers the secret to the process through an environment variable, mounted file, sidecar, or other boundary.

9
00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:17.602
The application reads a local value, while the platform handles retrieval.

10
00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:23.410
Injection can make application code simpler, but it does not remove lifecycle concerns.

11
00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:33.246
File permissions, environment exposure, refresh behavior, and rotation still matter. If a mounted secret changes, the service may need to reload it.

12
00:01:33.246 --> 00:01:40.061
If an environment variable changes, an already running process usually will not see it without restart.

13
00:01:40.061 --> 00:01:44.940
A useful Go pattern is to hide provider specifics behind a small interface.

14
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:52.762
For example, application code can depend on a SecretProvider with a Get method that accepts a context and a logical name.

15
00:01:52.762 --> 00:01:58.648
The production implementation calls the managed store. Tests use a fake implementation.

16
00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:08.716
This keeps business logic from importing provider SDKs everywhere and makes timeout behavior easier to test. Client initialization also deserves care.

17
00:02:08.716 --> 00:02:18.552
Use the platform's recommended workload identity or service account path instead of embedding long-lived provider credentials in the application.

18
00:02:18.552 --> 00:02:23.664
Grant only the secret paths or names the service needs. Set explicit timeouts.

19
00:02:23.664 --> 00:02:29.937
Avoid unbounded retries during startup, because a failing provider can make deployments hang.

20
00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:35.823
Log the secret name or provider path only if it is approved to disclose; never log the value.

21
00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:39.927
Managed stores are not a substitute for engineering judgment.

22
00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:48.214
They centralize control, but your Go service still has to retrieve, validate, use, cache, rotate, and report failures safely.

23
00:02:48.214 --> 00:02:51.699
Provider-neutral design also helps with migration.

24
00:02:51.699 --> 00:02:59.367
A team may start with platform-injected files, move to a cloud secrets service, and later adopt dynamic credentials.

25
00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:05.950
If business code depends on a small interface, those changes stay near the infrastructure boundary.

26
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:15.398
If provider SDK objects spread through the codebase, rotation policy, retries, and error handling become harder to change consistently later.
